<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silistra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Durostorum, Drastar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Silistra is a town on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. As Roman Durostorum it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> base on the Danube frontier, and as Byzantine Dorostolon and Bulgarian Drastar it was an important fortress and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat. It was the scene of the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> in 971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silistra.jpg" alt="Silistra" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juju13620 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silistra_River_Danube.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
